{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And this is the matter of the release.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory said: the lender says to the borrower, 'I am releasing.' When the borrower brings his money after the shemittah has passed over it, if the borrower says to him, 'I wish to repay you,' the lender may accept it from him, because Scripture did not mention regarding the borrower that he may not repay his debt, as it did regarding interest, where it withheld from both of them, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 23:20): ״לא תשיך לאחיך״ — 'you shall not exact interest from your brother' — which is a prohibition upon the borrower; but regarding shemittah it prohibited only the lender, that he may not press the borrower to repay and may not conduct himself as a master to compel him to pay, because the Jubilee belongs to the Master of all, may He be exalted, and this is the meaning of 'for it is called shemittah to HaShem'; but if the borrower brings it of his own will, it is permitted to the lender to accept from his hand. And our Rabbis of blessed memory said: one who repays a debt to a lender after the seventh year has passed over it — the spirit of the Sages is pleased with him. And if he does not repay, it is permitted for the lender to shame him and say to him, 'See that you are eating my money for nothing.' And likewise the borrower may waive the nullification of the seventh year at the time of the loan and say, 'On condition that you not release it in the seventh year I have lent this money,' for any condition concerning money is valid. But he should not say, 'that the seventh year not release it,' for that implies that the law of shemittah should not apply to his loan, and this is not within his power. And the lender is permitted to demand his debt throughout the entire shemittah year, for it does not release until its end, as it is stated: 'מקץ' — 'at the end.' And one who hands his debt document to the court beforehand — it is no longer released, for it is already pressed and standing, and there is no concern of 'לא יגוש' — 'he shall not press'; but once the sun has set on the head of the year at the conclusion of the seventh year, he is no longer permitted to demand it unless he stipulated with him from the outset. And orphans do not need this condition, for the court of every generation is the father of orphans, and it is as though their documents have come into their hands and they have stood them upon the borrower's property. And even a loan that involves an oath — shemittah releases, as we learned in the chapter 'Kol HaNishba'in': 'and the seventh year releases the oath,' because it is stated 'וזה דבר השמטה' — 'and this is the matter of the release' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory said: this teaches that even a verbal matter is released. And the remission of monies applies in every place and at every time by rabbinic decree, in order that the law of shemittah not be forgotten from Israel; only, Scripture suspended the remission of monies upon the remission of lands, even though the latter is an obligation of the body and is fit to apply in every place, as it is stated (Exodus 23:11): ״תשמטנה ונטשתה״ — 'you shall let it rest and leave it' — and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'the verse speaks of two shemittot — one, the remission of land, and one, the remission of monies: at the time that you release land in the Land, you release monies outside the Land.' Nevertheless, it is a rabbinic enactment, and so we see that the Sages of the Talmud would make a prozbol in Babylonia in order to escape the nullification of the seventh year. And one who lends to his fellow for ten years — the first shemittah does not release, since the time for repayment has not yet arrived, but rather the second shemittah."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:48:30.220613541Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}